Gandolfo"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/158322"} "32 from 16 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Defending Olympic champions " [:a {:href "/athletes/40215"} "Aleksandr Berkutov"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/40379"} "Yury Tyukalov"] " of the Soviet Union were the heavy favorites to retain their title in Rome, as they were on their fourth of five consecutive European Championship crowns and had won the Double Sculls Challenge Cup at the Henley Royal Regatta (then considered the equivalent of a World Championship) in 1957 and 1958. Great Britain had the potential to put up a legitimate challenge, as their entrants, " [:a {:href "/athletes/37222"} "Nicholas Birkmyre"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37348"} "George Justicz"] ", earned the first of their four Double Sculls Challenge Cup victories in 1960. The United States had an interesting entry that included " [:a {:href "/athletes/40733"} "Jack Kelly, Jr."] ", who had appeared in three previous Olympics as a single sculler."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The shock in the opening round came when " [:a {:href "/athletes/40009"} "Václav Kozák"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/40064"} "Pavel Schmidt"] " of Czechoslovakia  won their heat half a second ahead of Berkutov and Tyukalov and well ahead of the Britons. Kozák and Schmidt were not unknowns, having been runners-up in the most recent European Championships, but their performance was surprising nonetheless. The Soviets managed to advance due to their performance in the repêchage, but Great Britain was eliminated. In the final, Czechoslovakia captured the gold medal with ease, finishing three seconds ahead of the Soviets, who had to struggle to keep the silver one out of the hands of the Swiss duo " [:a {:href "/athletes/39741"} "Ernst Hürlimann"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39758"} "Rolf Larcher"] ", a feat that they accomplished with only a tenth of a second to spare."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr 